1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,765
DEEPAK: On October fourteenth, I asked
Claude to shorten one subtitle line,

2
00:00:03,805 --> 00:00:07,513
DEEPAK: so a viewer could read it in time.
It fit. It kept both negatives.

3
00:00:07,553 --> 00:00:10,750
DEEPAK: And it still shifted the tone of
what the character said.

4
00:00:10,792 --> 00:00:12,502
SKEPTIC: It's one line. Who cares?

5
00:00:14,792 --> 00:00:16,299
DEEPAK: Anyone who ships subtitles.

6
00:00:16,339 --> 00:00:20,138
DEEPAK: Streaming platforms set hard
limits on how fast a line can be read,

7
00:00:20,178 --> 00:00:23,576
DEEPAK: and delivery guides say files that
break them get sent back.

8
00:00:23,616 --> 00:00:27,873
DEEPAK: In the tools I checked, shortening
a line is still something a person does,

9
00:00:27,913 --> 00:00:28,962
DEEPAK: one line at a time.

10
00:00:29,042 --> 00:00:31,410
DEEPAK: So CueGate is the tool I'm going
to build.

11
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:33,762
DEEPAK: You give it a subtitle file and
the film.

12
00:00:33,802 --> 00:00:37,031
DEEPAK: It will hand back a file where
every line fits the rules,

13
00:00:37,071 --> 00:00:41,332
DEEPAK: and anything it can't fix safely
will go back to a person, with the reason.

14
00:00:41,375 --> 00:00:44,815
SKEPTIC: And why would I trust it more
than the AI button I already have?

15
00:00:45,375 --> 00:00:48,748
DEEPAK: Because it won't just claim the
line still means the same thing.

16
00:00:48,788 --> 00:00:52,055
DEEPAK: It will measure how often it's
wrong, and publish that number.

17
00:00:53,225 --> 00:00:55,085
SKEPTIC: Hasn't someone already done this?

18
00:00:55,125 --> 00:00:55,508
DEEPAK: Partly.

19
00:00:55,548 --> 00:00:59,799
DEEPAK: Subtitle Edit and Editsub both
have an AI action that shortens the line

20
00:00:59,839 --> 00:01:04,333
DEEPAK: you're on. EZTitles runs automated
checks that flag reading-speed problems.

21
00:01:04,373 --> 00:01:07,355
DEEPAK: In all three, a person picks the
line and decides.

22
00:01:07,416 --> 00:01:10,235
DEEPAK: Researchers have worked on length
too. In 2019,

23
00:01:10,275 --> 00:01:14,615
DEEPAK: a team in Trento and at Amazon
showed a translation model can be steered

24
00:01:14,655 --> 00:01:19,056
DEEPAK: to write shorter output. In 2020,
a Trento team found that for subtitles,

25
00:01:19,096 --> 00:01:21,611
DEEPAK: hitting forty-two characters isn't
enough.

26
00:01:21,651 --> 00:01:24,226
DEEPAK: Timing and the rhythm of speech
matter too.

27
00:01:24,291 --> 00:01:25,271
SKEPTIC: So what's missing?

28
00:01:26,916 --> 00:01:28,572
DEEPAK: What they got was shorter text.

29
00:01:28,612 --> 00:01:32,345
DEEPAK: What none of the tools I checked
publish is how often that shortening

30
00:01:32,385 --> 00:01:35,298
DEEPAK: changes meaning. That's the gap
CueGate will go after:

31
00:01:35,338 --> 00:01:38,306
DEEPAK: a gate over the whole file, and a
number you can check.

32
00:01:40,355 --> 00:01:41,335
SKEPTIC: Okay. How?

33
00:01:41,375 --> 00:01:44,315
DEEPAK: First, the data. The English
subtitles of Tears of Steel,

34
00:01:44,355 --> 00:01:46,982
DEEPAK: an open Blender film. The film is
Creative Commons,

35
00:01:47,022 --> 00:01:49,545
DEEPAK: but the subtitle file's own
licence isn't stated,

36
00:01:49,585 --> 00:01:52,577
DEEPAK: so I'll confirm it before it goes
in the repo. If I can't,

37
00:01:52,617 --> 00:01:55,192
DEEPAK: I'll write my own dialogue and
label it synthetic.

38
00:01:55,232 --> 00:01:58,957
DEEPAK: Clean files rarely break rules, so
I'll squeeze their timing on purpose.

39
00:01:58,997 --> 00:02:02,095
DEEPAK: That gives me failures where I
still know the original line.

40
00:02:02,166 --> 00:02:06,007
DEEPAK: Then the pipeline. Read the file
and turn every time into exact frames.

41
00:02:06,047 --> 00:02:09,613
DEEPAK: Check every rule. Fix timing
first, because that changes no words.

42
00:02:09,653 --> 00:02:13,494
DEEPAK: Only the lines timing can't save
go to a small open model running on my

43
00:02:13,534 --> 00:02:17,428
DEEPAK: laptop, with an exact character
budget and a list of words it must keep.

44
00:02:17,468 --> 00:02:20,106
DEEPAK: Every answer comes back through
the same checker.

45
00:02:20,166 --> 00:02:21,746
SKEPTIC: And when the model gets it wrong?

46
00:02:22,791 --> 00:02:26,611
DEEPAK: It gets three tries, each with the
reason it failed. After that,

47
00:02:26,651 --> 00:02:30,771
DEEPAK: the line stays untouched and goes
to a person. The gate fails closed.

48
00:02:30,833 --> 00:02:32,503
SKEPTIC: Do you actually have what you
need?

49
00:02:33,458 --> 00:02:35,527
DEEPAK: No API key, and none needed to
start:

50
00:02:35,567 --> 00:02:38,205
DEEPAK: the rule checks and timing fixes
need no model.

51
00:02:38,245 --> 00:02:40,884
DEEPAK: Compute is my sixteen-inch MacBook
Pro, M3 Pro,

52
00:02:40,924 --> 00:02:43,221
DEEPAK: thirty-six gigabytes of memory, no
cloud.

53
00:02:43,261 --> 00:02:45,329
DEEPAK: The subtitle licence isn't
confirmed;

54
00:02:45,369 --> 00:02:47,324
DEEPAK: synthetic dialogue is the
fallback.

55
00:02:47,364 --> 00:02:50,914
DEEPAK: The models will be public models
that download and run locally.

56
00:02:50,954 --> 00:02:53,878
DEEPAK: I'll pick small ones here, after
freeing disk space.

57
00:02:54,888 --> 00:02:56,918
SKEPTIC: Last one. How will you know it
worked?

58
00:02:56,958 --> 00:03:00,543
DEEPAK: I'll run four versions on the same
lines. A dumb cut at the budget.

59
00:03:00,583 --> 00:03:03,086
DEEPAK: A single "shorten this" call to
the same model.

60
00:03:03,126 --> 00:03:05,899
DEEPAK: CueGate without its meaning check.
And full CueGate.

61
00:03:05,939 --> 00:03:08,550
DEEPAK: Then I'll label a hundred
shortened lines myself,

62
00:03:08,590 --> 00:03:10,768
DEEPAK: without knowing which version
wrote them.

63
00:03:10,833 --> 00:03:13,537
DEEPAK: It worked if full CueGate ships
zero rule breaks,

64
00:03:13,577 --> 00:03:16,506
DEEPAK: and changes meaning less often
than that single call.

65
00:03:16,546 --> 00:03:19,082
DEEPAK: It failed if it changes meaning
just as often,

66
00:03:19,122 --> 00:03:20,818
DEEPAK: however clean the timing looks.

67
00:03:20,858 --> 00:03:24,739
DEEPAK: And if it sends most lines back to
a person, it saved nobody any time.

68
00:03:24,779 --> 00:03:26,643
DEEPAK: I'll report that as a failure too.

69
00:03:26,708 --> 00:03:27,838
SKEPTIC: And who checks the checker?

70
00:03:29,333 --> 00:03:33,345
DEEPAK: I'll count how often the meaning
check waved through a line I marked as

71
00:03:33,385 --> 00:03:36,713
DEEPAK: changed. That number goes in the
report, even if it's ugly.

72
00:03:36,791 --> 00:03:37,971
SKEPTIC: You say "will" a lot.

73
00:03:39,416 --> 00:03:43,764
DEEPAK: Because most of it doesn't exist
yet. Today there's the plan, the research,

74
00:03:43,804 --> 00:03:46,923
DEEPAK: and that one dated Claude reply.
No CueGate code runs.

75
00:03:46,963 --> 00:03:49,146
DEEPAK: Everything else is "will", on
purpose.

76
00:03:49,208 --> 00:03:54,158
DEEPAK: CueGate. Subtitles that fit the
frame, or don't ship. I'm Deepak Kumaran.
